    "faq": {
      "type": "bottom-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "f1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What is nattokinase?",
            "content": "<p>Nattokinase is a serine-protease enzyme extracted from natto, a traditional Japanese fermented soybean food. Discovered in Tokyo in 1980 by Dr. Hiroyuki Sumi, it's been studied for over 40 years for its activity on fibrin — the protein involved in blood clotting and the matrix of arterial plaque.</p>"
          }
        },
        "f2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How does nattokinase work in the body?",
            "content": "<p>Nattokinase is a serine protease — a protein-cutting enzyme — that acts directly on fibrin and inactivates PAI-1, the body's natural brake on fibrin clearance. In a 12-month human trial (Chen 2022, n=1,062), cardiovascular markers including carotid plaque area, arterial wall thickness, LDL, and HDL improved significantly at 6,000–12,000 FU/day with zero adverse events.</p>"
          }
        },
        "f3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why 4,000 FU per capsule? How many should I take?",
            "content": "<p>The largest clinical study to date — Chen et al. (2022, Frontiers in Cardiovascular Medicine, n=1,062, 12 months) — concludes the clinically active range is <strong>6,000–12,000 FU per day</strong>. We picked 4,000 FU per capsule so you can dose to your protocol with your clinician: 1 capsule = 4,000 FU (entry); 2 capsules = ~8,000 FU (within active range); 3 capsules = ~12,000 FU (matches Chen 2022 range).</p>"
          }
        },
        "f4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Are there clinical studies on nattokinase and spike protein?",
            "content": "<p>Yes — two published studies: Tanikawa et al. (2022, Molecules) showed in vitro that purified nattokinase degrades the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein in 60–180 minutes. Grixti, Pretorius & Kell (2024, preprint) showed nattokinase degrades fibrinaloid microclots associated with Long COVID in vitro. PMC links: <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9457822/\">PMC9457822</a> (Tanikawa). Both are in vitro / mechanistic studies — not human RCTs.</p>"
          }
        },
        "f5": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is it safe with Eliquis, Xarelto, warfarin, or aspirin?",
            "content": "<p><strong>Talk to your prescribing physician before starting.</strong> Nattokinase has fibrinolytic activity, so the conversation matters. We removed vitamin K2 specifically because K2 interacts with vitamin-K-antagonists like warfarin. Chen 2022 reported no adverse events across 1,062 adults at 10,800 FU/day for 12 months.</p>"
          }
        },
        "f6": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How many servings per bottle?",
            "content": "<p>Each bottle of Spike Detox (Pure) contains 30 capsules. At 1 capsule/day (4,000 FU entry dose) that is a 30-day supply. At 3 capsules/day (12,000 FU — matching Chen 2022 range) that is a 10-day supply. The 90-cap bundle is the most economical for users running 2–3 capsules/day.</p>"
          }
        },
        "f7": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is it allergen-free?",
            "content": "<p>Nattokinase is derived from fermented soybeans — individuals with soy allergies should consult their physician before use. The capsule shell is vegan (HPMC). There are no gluten, dairy, egg, nut, or shellfish ingredients. No synthetic dyes or fillers.</p>"
          }
        },
        "f8": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "When should I take it?",
            "content": "<p>On an empty stomach, ideally 30 minutes before a meal or before bed. Food can reduce enzyme activity. Many people split into morning + evening doses if taking 2–3 capsules/day. Separate from PRN nitroglycerin by at least 2 hours.</p>"
          }
        },
        "f9": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Where can I read the studies?",
            "content": "<p>Chen et al. 2022 — <em>Effective management of atherosclerosis progress and hyperlipidemia with nattokinase</em>, <em>Frontiers in Cardiovascular Medicine</em>. <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9458005/\">PMC9458005</a></p><p>Tanikawa et al. 2022 — spike-protein degradation in vitro. <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9457822/\">PMC9457822</a></p><p>Grixti, Pretorius & Kell 2024 (preprint) — fibrinaloid microclot degradation. <a href=\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.04.06.588397v1\">bioRxiv preprint</a></p>"
          }
        },
        "f10": {
          "type": "faq",
